Well I certainly prefer some other way than what is there right now in this case :).
For c# this brings a really weird double underscore and a mix of upper/lowercasing. It really feels like a mismatch.
Underscores for gdscript I guess would make sense in a way you say though I don't think underscores would be popular in C# especially considering current format.

Standard C# example just in case someone pops in who hasn't used C# in Godot for a comparison:
```
    public override void _PhysicsProcess(float delta)
    {
        base._PhysicsProcess(delta);
        KinematicBody kb = new();
        kb.MoveAndSlide(..);
    }
```

Since slashes are not valid for member identifiers, the C# bindings generator replaces them with two underscores in 3.x:

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/653687711730ae1475e9183df69e730286e6eee0/modules/mono/editor/bindings_generator.cpp#L2281

This was changed in https://github.com/godotengine/godot/commit/af4acb5b1163089fe727000b39cefb4fc799f1db and now in Godot 4.0+ these properties are not generated in C# classes. This change was made because we assume properties with slashes in their name are only meant to be used in the inspector so they are not accessible from C# scripting, I don't think you can access these properties from GDScript either so I think that assumption is correct.

If they are supposed to accessible from scripting but still want to group them there is the `ADD_GROUP` macro that we could use:

```C++
ADD_GROUP("Geometry", "geometry_");
ADD_PROPERTY(PropertyInfo(Variant::INT, "geometry_parsed_geometry_type", PROPERTY_HINT_ENUM, "Mesh Instances,Static Colliders,Both"), "set_parsed_geometry_type", "get_parsed_geometry_type");
ADD_PROPERTY(PropertyInfo(Variant::INT, "geometry_collision_mask", PROPERTY_HINT_LAYERS_3D_PHYSICS), "set_collision_mask", "get_collision_mask");
ADD_PROPERTY(PropertyInfo(Variant::INT, "geometry_source_geometry_mode", PROPERTY_HINT_ENUM, "Navmesh Children, Group With Children, Group Explicit"), "set_source_geometry_mode", "get_source_geometry_mode");
ADD_PROPERTY(PropertyInfo(Variant::STRING, "geometry_source_group_name"), "set_source_group_name", "get_source_group_name");
```

This adds all the properties starting with the `geometry_` prefix to a group called `Geometry`.

Also, related: #17558

@smix8 It shouldn't require a lot of setters and getters, just something like this (untested):
```cpp
#ifndef DISABLE_DEPRECATED
bool NavigationMesh::_set(const StringName &p_name, Variant &p_value) {
	String name = p_name;
	if (name.find("/") != -1) {
		// Compatibility with pre-3.5 "category/path" property names.
		name = name.replace("/", "_");
		set(name, p_value);
		return true;
	}
	return false;
}

bool NavigationMesh::_get(const StringName &p_name, Variant &r_ret) const {
	String name = p_name;
	if (name.find("/") != -1) {
		// Compatibility with pre-3.5 "category/path" property names.
		name = name.replace("/", "_");
		r_ret = get(name);
		return true;
	}
	return false;
}
#endif // DISABLE_DEPRECATED
```

If it works, the result should be that opening a 3.4 scene with e.g. `cell/size = 4.0` serialized should properly convert it to `cell_size = 4.0` instead of losing the value.
